  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 247 AM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nSunday, an upper level trough is expected to track across the \nwestern United States, bringing another stream of Pacific moisture \nacross the region. Throughout the day, a low pressure system is \nexpected to develop and deepen across eastern Colorado. With the \nmoisture streaming into the region, expecting cloud cover to persist \nthroughout the day, which will limit high temperatures mostly to the \nupper 50s to lower 60s. While the low develops throughout the day, \nnot expecting much in the way of precipitation, but may see some \ngusty southerly winds as the low deepens. However, by the evening, \nexpecting that the low will begin to lift, bringing precipitation \nchances across the region. The low is expected to slowly track \nacross the region, keeping rain showers around on throughout the day \nMonday, with highs again in the 50s to lower 60s. As a shortwave \ndevelops in the northern jet stream, expecting another low pressure \nto track east across the Dakotas, which will bring a cold front into \nthe region. There is still some slight timing differences in models, \nbut appears that the front should track through sometime Tuesday \nmorning. As the front tracks through, this should give one more shot \nof precipitation across the region, with cooler temperatures \nsettling in. As of now, temperatures are expected to remain just \nabove freezing, and appears that any precipitation falling should \nremain liquid precipitation. Showers may linger through the day on \nTuesday, especially areas east of Highway 83. However, by Tuesday \nevening, expecting that precipitation should track out of the \nregion. \n\nThrough the rest of the week, cooler air remains in the wake of the \ncold front. Highs should drop back into the lower 50s, and \npotentially into the upper 40s by the end of the week. Lows are also \nexpected to drop back around freezing each night. This is not too \nout of the ordinary for early November. In fact, local climatology \nshows that our typical highs are in the mid to upper 50s, with lows \nin the mid 20s across the region for early November. Precipitation \nchances also appear low Wednesday through late week, but will be \nkeeping an eye on a potential upper level disturbance next weekend, \nwhich may bring additional precipitation chances.",
